---
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import SEO from "../components/SEO.astro";

const questions = [
  {
    id: "state-moral-legitimacy",
    number: "01",
    title: "Is the State morally legitimate?",
    answer: [
      "No. Even the most benevolent State remains coercive because it claims authority over everyone within a territory, whether they consent or not",
      "If individuals were free to join or leave, it would be a voluntary association rather than a State. State rule is not optional; coercion is therefore inherent in the institution",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "state-criminal-organization",
    number: "02",
    title: "Is the State a criminal organization?",
    answer: [
      "Yes, in a normative sense. The State claims powers—compulsory jurisdiction, taxation, and enforcement—that would be considered coercive if exercised by a voluntary association",
      "Legality does not by itself confer moral legitimacy on those acts",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "government-efficiency",
    number: "03",
    title: "Can government ever be efficient?",
    answer: [
      "No—not in the institutional sense relevant here. A public agency may perform a particular task well, but that is not the same as government being an efficient institution",
      "State provision faces structural limits of information, economic calculation, incentives, competition, exit, and error correction. Those limits can be reduced, but not eliminated",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "functions-of-government",
    number: "04",
    title: "What functions should government perform?",
    answer: [
      "There is no predefined list. Functions must be examined individually rather than inherited from convention, constitutions, or political tradition",
      "The relevant question is whether coercive necessity can be established for the function itself",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "burden-of-proof-coercion",
    number: "05",
    title: "Who must prove that coercion is necessary?",
    answer: [
      "Whoever seeks to impose it. Liberty is the baseline, and the burden of proof falls on power",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "democracy-legitimizes-coercion",
    number: "06",
    title: "Does democracy legitimize State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "No. Democratic procedures can determine who exercises power and under what rules, but they do not turn compulsory decisions into consent",
      "Majority approval is a method of collective decision-making, not a moral exemption from the need to justify coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "essential-services-state-coercion",
    number: "07",
    title: "Do essential services justify State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "No. A service can be essential, valuable, or socially beneficial without making compulsory provision necessary",
      "The value of an end and the justification for coercive means are separate questions",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "public-goods-government-provision",
    number: "08",
    title: "Do public goods require government provision?",
    answer: [
      "No. Non-excludability and non-rivalry describe a structure of the good, not an institutional conclusion",
      "Voluntary provision can still be possible. Samuelson formalized the structure of collective consumption goods in <em>The Pure Theory of Public Expenditure</em>; it does not follow that the State must provide them",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "free-rider-state-coercion",
    number: "09",
    title: "Does the free-rider problem justify State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "No. The fact that someone can benefit without contributing does not show that a function cannot be sustained voluntarily",
      "Free riding becomes relevant when the same incentive problem produces persistent failure across materially different arrangements",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "failure-voluntary-provision",
    number: "10",
    title: "When does voluntary provision actually fail?",
    answer: [
      "When materially different arrangements fail persistently because the same incentive structure prevents the function from being sustained",
      "One failed provider, underprovision, or a temporary absence is not enough",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "schmidtz-public-goods-free-riders",
    number: "11",
    title: "What does David Schmidtz argue about public goods and free riders?",
    answer: [
      "In <em>The Limits of Government</em>, Schmidtz shows that public goods and free riders do not by themselves justify state authority and that some voluntary arrangements can work",
      "Minimal Tolerance adds a stricter requirement: persistent collapse of voluntary provision must be established before coercion can be considered",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "market-failure-government-intervention",
    number: "12",
    title: "Do market failures justify government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "No. Categories such as natural monopoly, imperfect information, concentration, coordination problems, or underprovision describe certain market conditions",
      "By themselves, they do not show that government intervention is necessary or superior to available alternatives",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "externalities-government-intervention",
    aliases: ["positive-externalities-government-intervention"],
    number: "13",
    title: "Do externalities justify government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Not by themselves. Positive externalities show that others benefit, but that does not prove government intervention is necessary. Pigouvian taxes or subsidies are proposed remedies, yet identifying a spillover is not the same as justifying coercion",
      "Negative externalities can involve harm to others, making the case stronger. But then the first questions are rights, liability, and compensation; an external cost alone does not create a general right of government intervention",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "libertarianism-state-coercion",
    number: "14",
    title: "Can a libertarian theory tolerate State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "Yes, exceptionally. Minimal Tolerance begins from liberty and rejects the moral legitimacy of coercion",
      "It differs from stricter libertarian positions only in allowing coercion to be tolerated when necessity leaves no viable voluntary alternative",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "hayek-government-power",
    number: "15",
    title: "What limits government power in Hayek's political theory?",
    answer: [
      "Hayek emphasizes the limits of knowledge, spontaneous order, general rules, and the rule of law",
      "Minimal Tolerance asks a prior question: which functions may be placed under coercive authority at all",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "buchanan-constitutional-political-economy",
    number: "16",
    title: "What limits government in Buchanan's constitutional political economy?",
    answer: [
      "Buchanan examines the constitutional rules within which collective decisions are made and the role of agreement",
      "Minimal Tolerance moves one step earlier by asking whether a function may be coercively assigned before its institutional design is chosen",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "rothbard-anarcho-capitalism-state-coercion",
    number: "17",
    title: "Does Rothbard's anarcho-capitalism reject all State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "Yes. Rothbard treats coercion as morally illegitimate whether it comes from the State or anyone else and rejects compulsory state provision",
      "Minimal Tolerance shares that rejection of moral legitimacy but departs from Rothbard by allowing exceptional tolerance of a coercive function when necessity is established",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "minarchism-defense-police-courts",
    number: "18",
    title: "Does minarchism justify a State limited to defense, police, and courts?",
    answer: [
      "Classical minarchism treats defense, police, and courts as the legitimate core functions of the State",
      "Minimal Tolerance rejects granting those functions privileged status by default. A smaller State is not morally exempt from the problem of coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "nozick-minimal-state-legitimacy",
    number: "19",
    title: "Is Nozick's minimal State morally legitimate?",
    answer: [
      "In <em>Anarchy, State, and Utopia</em>, Nozick defends a minimal state as morally legitimate",
      "Minimal Tolerance rejects that conclusion: reducing the scope of the State does not change the moral status of its coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "absence-voluntary-alternatives-state-necessary",
    number: "20",
    title: "Does the absence of voluntary alternatives prove the State is necessary?",
    answer: [
      "No. The fact that a function is not being provided voluntarily does not, by itself, give the State a right to provide it coercively",
      "A legal monopoly, state provision, or restrictive regulation may also have prevented alternatives from emerging",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "economic-inequality-state-coercion",
    number: "21",
    title: "Does economic inequality justify State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "No. Inequality is a difference in outcomes; by itself, it is neither a moral wrong nor a moral good",
      "People can become unequal through work, choices, luck, inheritance, privilege, fraud, or aggression. Where there is wrongdoing, the wrong is the violation—not inequality itself",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "poverty-state-coercion",
    number: "22",
    title: "Does poverty justify State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "No. Poverty can create powerful moral reasons to help, but need alone does not establish a right to compel others to provide or finance assistance",
      "The importance of relieving poverty and the justification of State coercion are separate questions",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "collective-action-government-intervention",
    number: "23",
    title: "Do collective-action problems require government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "No. Collective-action problems can often be addressed through contracts, associations, firms, clubs, insurance, charities, norms, and other voluntary arrangements",
      "Difficulty, underprovision, or imperfect coordination does not prove that coercion is necessary",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "keynesian-economics-government-intervention",
    number: "24",
    title: "Does Keynesian economics justify government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Keynesian economics argues that government spending or other stabilization policies can improve outcomes during recessions or weak aggregate demand",
      "That may support a case for effectiveness, but effectiveness is not the same as justification. Taxation, compulsory financing, and other coercive means must still be justified separately",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "rawls-theory-justice-redistribution",
    number: "25",
    title: "Does Rawls's theory of justice justify redistribution?",
    answer: [
      "Rawls provides a moral case for institutions that regulate social and economic inequalities",
      "Even if that ideal is accepted, it does not by itself settle whether redistribution may be imposed coercively. The end and the coercive means require separate justification",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "musgrave-functions-government-intervention",
    number: "26",
    title: "Do Musgrave's functions of government justify State intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Musgrave's allocation, distribution, and stabilization branches classify major functions of public finance, but classification is not justification",
      "Each function must still show why State coercion is necessary. Economic usefulness or social desirability alone is not enough",
    ],
  },
];

const stripHtml = (value) =>
  value.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, "");

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: questions.map((question) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: question.title,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      "@type": "Answer",
      text: question.answer.map(stripHtml).join(" "),
    },
  })),
};
---

<!doctype html>

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />

    <meta
      name="viewport"
      content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"
    />

    <SEO
      title="What Should Government Be Allowed to Do? | Minimal Tolerance"
      description="When is government intervention justified? Explore State coercion, public goods, market failure, inequality, externalities, Keynes, Rawls, and more"
      lang="en"
    />

    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)}
    ></script>
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main>
      <!-- HERO -->

      <section class="hero questions-page-hero">
        <div class="hero-content hero-sequence">
          <p class="eyebrow">
            THE QUESTIONS
          </p>

          <h1>
            What should the State<br />
            be allowed to do?
          </h1>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- CENTRAL STATEMENT -->

      <section class="statement">
        <blockquote class="reveal reveal-scale">
          No one has an inherent right to rule another
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <!-- KEY QUESTIONS -->

      <section class="questions-index">
        <div class="questions-container">
          <p class="eyebrow reveal key-questions-label">
            KEY QUESTIONS
          </p>

          <nav
            class="questions-index-grid stagger"
            aria-label="Questions about government, coercion, public goods, and political power"
          >
            {
              questions.map((question) => (
                <a
                  href={`#${question.id}`}
                  class="question-index-card reveal"
                >
                  <span class="question-index-number">
                    {question.number}
                  </span>

                  <span class="question-index-title">
                    {question.title}
                  </span>
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </nav>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- QUESTIONS -->

      <section class="questions-content">
        <div class="questions-container">
          {
            questions.map((question) => (
              <article
                id={question.id}
                class="question-section"
              >
                {question.aliases?.map((alias) => (
                  <span id={alias} class="anchor-alias" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                ))}
                <div class="question-heading reveal reveal-left">
                  <span class="question-number">
                    {question.number}
                  </span>

                  <h2>
                    {question.title}
                  </h2>
                </div>

                <div class="question-copy reveal reveal-right">
                  {question.answer.map((paragraph) => (
                    <p set:html={paragraph}></p>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- FINAL CTA -->

      <section class="questions-cta">
        <div class="questions-container questions-cta-content">
          <div class="reveal reveal-left">
            <p class="eyebrow">
              THE THEORY OF MINIMAL TOLERANCE
            </p>

            <h2>
              The burden of proof belongs to power
            </h2>

            <p>
              Explore the criterion that determines when a function can
              pass the test of coercive necessity without turning
              tolerance into legitimacy
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="questions-cta-buttons reveal reveal-right">
            <a href="/theory" class="primary">
              Explore the Theory
            </a>

            <a href="/theorem" class="secondary">
              Examine the Theorem
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <Footer />

    <style>
      /*
       * Force a true two-column grid here so each pair of questions
       * shares the exact same row height.
       */
      .key-questions-label {
        color: #a07d37 !important;
        font-size: 1.15rem;
        letter-spacing: 0.14em;
        margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
      }

      .questions-index-grid {
        display: grid !important;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)) !important;
        grid-auto-flow: row !important;
        gap: 0 !important;
        align-items: stretch !important;
      }

      .question-index-card {
        box-sizing: border-box !important;
        display: grid !important;
        grid-template-columns: 3.1rem minmax(0, 1fr) !important;
        align-items: start !important;
        align-content: start !important;
        column-gap: 1rem !important;
        width: 100% !important;
        height: 100% !important;
        min-height: 0 !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 1rem 1.15rem 1rem 0 !important;
        transition:
          color 220ms ease,
          background 220ms ease,
          transform 220ms ease;
      }

      .question-index-card:nth-child(odd) {
        border-right: 1px solid var(--line, rgba(20, 30, 40, 0.14)) !important;
        padding-right: 1.4rem !important;
      }

      .question-index-card:nth-child(even) {
        padding-left: 2rem !important;
      }

      .question-index-number,
      .question-index-title {
        align-self: start !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
      }

      .question-index-number {
        line-height: 1.28 !important;
        transition: transform 220ms ease;
      }

      .question-index-title {
        display: block !important;
        line-height: 1.28;
      }

      /*
       * Keep the index type exactly at the approved size.
       */
      .question-index-title {
        font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1vw, 1.08rem);
      }

      /*
       * Developed-question title: 10% smaller than the previous version.
       * Right-side answer size is untouched.
       */
      .question-number {
        display: inline-block;
        transform: scale(1.50);
        transform-origin: left top;
      }

      .question-heading h2 {
        font-size: clamp(1.08rem, 1.62vw, 1.62rem);
        line-height: 1.12;
      }

      .question-section {
        position: relative;
        overflow: hidden;
      }

      .anchor-alias {
        position: absolute;
        top: 0;
        left: 0;
        width: 0;
        height: 0;
      }

      @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
        .question-index-card:hover {
          transform: translateY(-2px);
        }

        .question-index-card:hover .question-index-number {
          transform: translateX(5px);
        }
      }

      @media (max-width: 900px) {
        .questions-index-grid {
          grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
        }

        .question-index-card,
        .question-index-card:nth-child(odd),
        .question-index-card:nth-child(even) {
          grid-template-columns: 2.75rem minmax(0, 1fr) !important;
          column-gap: 0.85rem !important;
          padding: 0.9rem 0 !important;
          border-right: 0 !important;
        }

        .question-index-title {
          font-size: 0.95rem;
        }

        .question-heading h2 {
          font-size: clamp(0.99rem, 3.33vw, 1.26rem);
        }

        .question-heading.reveal-left,
        .question-copy.reveal-right {
          transform: translateY(24px);
        }

        .question-heading.reveal-left.is-visible,
        .question-copy.reveal-right.is-visible {
          transform: translateY(0);
        }
      }

      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .question-index-card,
        .question-index-number {
          transition: none;
        }
      }
    </style>

    <script is:inline>
      const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia(
        "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
      ).matches;

      const revealElements =
        document.querySelectorAll(".reveal");

      if (reduceMotion || !("IntersectionObserver" in window)) {
        revealElements.forEach((element) => {
          element.classList.add("is-visible");
        });
      } else {
        const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries, observer) => {
            entries.forEach((entry) => {
              if (!entry.isIntersecting) return;

              entry.target.classList.add("is-visible");
              observer.unobserve(entry.target);
            });
          },
          {
            threshold: 0.12,
            rootMargin: "0px 0px -40px 0px",
          }
        );

        revealElements.forEach((element) => {
          revealObserver.observe(element);
        });
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
